FINAL ANSWER:
Troy Goode and Douglas Christopher Wilson own most of the surviving code, once the formatting commit is excluded. I counted lines per author at HEAD with `git blame -w -M -C`, so whitespace changes and moved code don't count as authorship. I ran it over all tracked text files.

The repo's formatting commit (`62628c0`, "reformat js with 4-space indent and double quotes") rewrote many lines and gave them to Fiona Formatter. `.git-blame-ignore-revs` lists that commit, but `blame.ignoreRevsFile` isn't set in this repo, so plain `git blame` still credits it. I ran the count both ways:

| Author | Raw blame | Ignoring the reformat commit |
|---|---|---|
| Troy Goode | 715 | 810 |
| Douglas Christopher Wilson | 454 | 629 |
| Fiona Formatter | 293 | not in the top 15 |
| Íñigo Marquínez Prado | 95 | 95 |
| Jon Church | 72 | 72 |
| Sebastian Beltran | 67 | 67 |
| Ulises Gascón | 52 | 52 |
| Ignacio Mangas | 44 | 44 |

- **Real owners:** Troy Goode (810 lines) and Douglas Christopher Wilson (629 lines) together own most of the code that survives. Everyone else owns 100 lines or fewer.
- **Formatter:** Fiona Formatter's 293 lines are only the reformat. They reflect no real authorship, and the ignore-revs file hands those lines back to the people who wrote them.
- **Inactive authors:** Íñigo Marquínez Prado owns 95 lines and last committed on 2024-05-14. Sebastian Beltran owns 67 lines and last committed on 2025-07-23.
- **Troy Goode's date:** Troy Goode's last commit is dated 2026-04-10, which doesn't fit a project of this age. I didn't investigate it.

To make normal `git blame` skip the formatting commit, run `git config blame.ignoreRevsFile .git-blame-ignore-revs`.

I didn't run the `knowledge_risk` tool, because it uses plain blame and would credit the reformat commit.

